  <!-- No-Copay Requirement -->
  <section class="msv__section">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <h2>The No-Copay Requirement Explained</h2>
      <p>One of the most misunderstood aspects of NLV insurance is the <strong>"sin copago"</strong> (no copayment) requirement. Let's clarify what this means and why it matters.</p>

      <h3>What "Sin Copago" Actually Means</h3>
      <p>In Spain's healthcare system, a "copago" is a per-visit charge or co-payment. For example, you might pay €3 for a doctor visit, €5 for a prescription, or €10 for an emergency room visit — amounts the patient must cover out-of-pocket.</p>
      <p><strong>Sin copago means:</strong> Your insurance covers 100% of these co-payments. You pay only your monthly premium — no additional charges when you visit a doctor, fill a prescription, or use a hospital.</p>

      <h3>Why Spanish Immigration Requires Sin Copago</h3>
      <p>Spain's immigration authorities specify this requirement for two reasons:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Financial proof:</strong> It proves you can afford healthcare without draining your bank account on every visit. The visa requires a minimum income, and sin copago ensures you won't deplete savings on medical costs.</li>
        <li><strong>Healthcare access:</strong> Without sin copago, some older applicants might avoid seeking treatment due to cost, straining public emergency services. Sin copago ensures you'll seek preventive care early.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Standard Spanish Insurance vs. NLV Requirements</h3>
      <p>Standard Spanish private insurance (for Spanish citizens and residents) often includes copagos of €3–10 per visit. This is acceptable for Spanish insurance purposes but <strong>NOT acceptable for NLV visa approval</strong>.</p>
      <p>Make sure your quote explicitly states "sin copago" or "zero copayment." If you're unsure, ask the insurer directly: "Is this policy sin copago for all covered services?"</p>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--info">
        <span class="msv__alert-icon">ℹ</span>
        <p><strong>Key distinction:</strong> "Sin copago" applies to normal medical services (doctor visits, prescriptions, hospital stays). It does NOT typically cover cosmetic procedures, dental work, or experimental treatments — but these aren't required for the visa anyway.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

