      <h2>Can You Switch Insurance Providers After Visa Approval?</h2>
      <p>Yes, you can switch health insurance providers after receiving your NLV visa. However, there are important considerations:</p>

      <h3>When Switching Makes Sense</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Better rate elsewhere:</strong> You found a cheaper provider offering similar coverage</li>
        <li><strong>Network issues:</strong> Current provider's doctor network is weak in your region</li>
        <li><strong>Service problems:</strong> Customer service or claims processing has been poor</li>
        <li><strong>Renewal time:</strong> When your annual policy renews (best time to switch without penalties)</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Considerations Before Switching</h3>
      <p><strong>Pre-existing condition clauses reset:</strong> If you switch providers, pre-existing conditions will be subject to a new 12-month waiting period with the new insurer. This can be problematic if you're mid-treatment.</p>
      <p><strong>Price increases at renewal:</strong> Some insurers offer lower introductory rates, then increase significantly at renewal. Switching every 2–3 years can keep costs down, but beware of the pre-existing condition reset.</p>
      <p><strong>Health changes:</strong> If your health deteriorated since your first policy, the new insurer may charge more or impose exclusions. Timing your switch during good health is strategic.</p>

      <h3>Best Practices for Switching</h3>
      <ol style="list-style: decimal; padding-left: 2rem; color: var(--black);">
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">Switch during contract renewal periods (typically anniversary dates) to avoid termination penalties.</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">Get quotes 2–3 months before renewal to compare rates and time your decision.</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">If you have pre-existing conditions, ensure the new policy has favorable waiting period terms before signing.</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">Keep proof of continuous coverage (letter from old insurer) to avoid gaps that could affect visa renewal.</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">Once switched, notify your visa renewal contact (lawyer or immigration agency) of the new policy. Immigration wants proof of continuous coverage.</li>
      </ol>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--info">
        <span class="msv__alert-icon">ℹ</span>
        <p><strong>Visa renewal tip:</strong> When you renew your NLV visa (typically every 1–2 years), you'll need to show proof of continuous health insurance. Switching to a significantly cheaper provider is acceptable as long as coverage remains uninterrupted.</p>
      </div>
